mod:mcpy_family_result;
// 2026-11-12 (landing 177d2b5, row 3: DEF-646's form 2). THE `mcpy` FAMILY'S RESULT IS ITS DESTINATION ARGUMENT'S VALUE. `mcpy`, `mmov` and `memset` hand back their
// first argument, so to the escape analysis the pointer one of them returns carries that argument's refs, is rooted where that argument is rooted, and borrows this
// frame where that argument does. A frame address cast to `wild`, handed to one of the three and cast back is the frame address still. One cell a face, its names
// suffixed with the cell's. Each face is accepted by row 2's commit (c9e3095), by main (169d6d4) and by b510126 (177d2b3). Each face's CAST TWIN, the same cast round
// trip with no builtin (`b.q = ((@mloc) =>! wild HPZ->) =>! HPZ->;`), is refused by every build, and this row gives the face its twin's codes. Each right control
// is in `mcpy_family_result_ok.npk`. The row closes 58 faces. It refuses 25 right programs, each named at the end of this comment, and every one fails closed.
// DEF-646's SIX LISTED FACES (Ailp-team_103's cells, of the third gate's fresh attack on 177d2b4; management_35's ruling of Wed 11-11 03:00 EST, "M is DEF-646").
// The result is put in a pointer member `b.q`, a caller's `List` pointer is stored through it (`(<-(b.q)).lp = hq;`), a view is taken through `mloc.lp`, the `List`
// is grown and the view is read: freed memory (236 at -O0 and -O2, 97 under the detector). `m_L_mc`, `m_L_mt`: `b` is a block's local (BORROW-015 at the growth).
// `m_F_mc`, `m_F_mt`: `b` is a `for` binding (BORROW-002 at the store). `m_P_mc`, `m_P_mt`: `b` is a consuming `pick` binding (BORROW-009 at the store, and in
// `m_P_mt` BORROW-002 too). `mc` puts the result straight into the member; `mt` takes it through a pointer local.
// Job B's `mc_` cells (nitpick-compiler_103's `mc_L_d`, `mc_L_t`, `mc_F_d`, `mc_F_t`, `mc_P_d` and `mc_P_t`) are these six again, the same text after the `mod:`
// line, and are not repeated here (management_39's ruling "Row 3's fresh attack is ruled" of Thu 11-12 04:58 EST, item 9; "the 04:58 ruling" below).
// TWELVE MORE, `mmov` AND `memset` (job B's): `mv_` and `ms_`, each in `L`, `F` and `P`, each `d` (straight into the member) and `t` (through a local). The same
// faults and the same reports as `mcpy`'s.
// THREE THROUGH A `wild` LOCAL (job B's): `mc_L_w`, `mv_L_w` and `ms_L_w` keep the result in `wild int8->:wr` and cast it after. BORROW-015 at the growth.
// TWELVE ON D-325's RULE B (job B's; management_37's ruling "Addendum: `nitpick-compiler_103`'s new faces" of Wed 11-11 20:49 EST: no new number, DEF-646's).
// `ret_mc`, `ret_mv` and `ret_ms` return the result over the function's own local (BORROW-001); accepted before, they read the dead frame. `st_mc`, `st_mv` and
// `st_ms` store the result over an inner block's local into a holder declared outside the block (BORROW-002); accepted before, they run 67 only because nothing
// reuses the slot. `retl_` and `stl_` of the three go through a `wild` local first.
// SIX MORE, WHERE THE RESULT LEAVES BY ANOTHER ROAD (nitpick-compiler_108's cells; management_39's ruling "Row 3's item 1 is ruled" of Thu 11-12 03:58 EST, item 2;
// "the 03:58 ruling" below). The family's result over a local's address is stored through a pointer parameter (`pst_loc`, and `pst_loc_mv` by `mmov`), stored into a
// slice parameter's element (`est_loc`), stored through a pointer to a pointer (`drf_loc`), put in a struct literal that is stored whole (`lit_loc`), or handed to a
// callee that stores it (`arg_loc`). Accepted before, each read a dead frame (230, 231 and 232 at -O0 on roads 0 to 2, 255 at -O2, 230 under the detector).
// BORROW-002 each.
// FOUR MORE, WHERE THE DESTINATION IS NOT WRITTEN AS THE ADDRESS ITSELF (management_39's "Addendum to row 3's item 1" of Thu 11-12 04:24 EST, item 2; "the 04:24
// addendum" below). The destination is a member of a local struct that holds the local's address (`est_wp_loc`), an element of a local array that holds it
// (`est_la_loc`), a local pointer that holds it (`est_lp_loc`; these three are nitpick-compiler_109's cells), or a local pointer made from the parameter's pointer
// and re-pointed at a local before the call (`est_lprp_loc`, management_39's). The result goes into a slice parameter's element. Accepted before, each read a dead
// frame as the six did. BORROW-002 each.
// FIFTEEN MORE, BUILT BY THE FRESH ATTACK ON THIS ROW (Ailp-team_111's cells; the 04:58 ruling, item 8). The family's result over a frame's address leaves by
// fifteen other roads. Accepted before, thirteen read a dead frame (230, 231 and 232, or 74, 75 and 76, at -O0 on roads 0 to 2; 255 at -O2; 230 or 74 under the
// detector), and two are faults no exit shows (`bs_hold` ran 67 and `df_st` 61).
// HANDED BACK, BORROW-001 each: `ar_ret` (in an array literal inside a struct literal that a helper returns), `ch_ret` (a chain: `mcpy`'s destination is `mmov`'s
// result over the local), `gen2` (a generic function hands back the family's result over its first parameter, and is called at `HPZ` with the local's address),
// `mvp_ret` (over the address of a `move` parameter), `rcv_val` (over the address of a by-value receiver), `wb_ret` (the local's address cast through
// `wild int8->` first), `wr_ret` (a user function hands back `mcpy`'s result, and is called with the local's address), `d390_fam` (the destination is `pb.q`,
// where a helper has stored the local's address through a local struct's `.ptr` member: DEF-390's road) and `mx_ret2` (over the address of a member of a pointer
// parameter's pointee, which is its caller's local: refused in the caller).
// STORED, BORROW-002 each: `bs_hold` (over an inner block's local, into a holder declared outside the block), `df_st` (the same store in a `defer` body), and
// `mx_fld`, `mx_fld_src` and `mx_pp` (a callee stores the result through one pointer parameter over another's pointee, and its caller hands it a local's address:
// the caller's call is refused, D-325). `lp_brk` stores it over a loop body's local into a holder declared outside the loop, leaves by `break` and hands the
// holder back: BORROW-002 and BORROW-001.
// Eleven have a twin among the attack's cells (`*_cw`, `d390_cw`) that every build refuses, ten of them with the face's code at the face's line. The eleventh,
// `mx_fld_cw`, is refused at the caller's call, as `mx_fld` is, and at the callee's store too: this row reports the caller's call alone for `mx_fld` and
// `mx_fld_src`, since the second look reads their destination as a parameter's alone (the 04:58 ruling, items 6 and 8).
// THE ROW'S PRICES: 25 RIGHT PROGRAMS, REFUSED (2026-11-12). Each ran 67 on every road on c9e3095, on main and on b510126. Fourteen are at parity with a twin that
// has no builtin's result in it, two are DEF-639's and nine are DEF-652's.
// AT PARITY, FOURTEEN (management_37's ruling "Job B is ruled" of Wed 11-11 22:05 EST, item 3: "The 12 controls it refuses are at parity with main's verdict on the
// cast spelling, and they're re-cut as refused-at-parity cells."; measured again on c9e3095 and on main by this row). Twelve are an `F` or `P` face with the
// growth left out. In these two binding kinds the STORE is refused whatever follows it, with or without the
// builtin, so the cast twin's control is refused by c9e3095 and by main with the same codes:
// `m_F_mcc_ok`, `mv_F_dc_ok`, `ms_F_dc_ok` (2026-11-12; the 22:05 ruling, item 3): BORROW-002, as their cast twin's control `m_F_cwc_ok` (Ailp-team_103's).
// `m_F_mtc_ok`, `mv_F_tc_ok`, `ms_F_tc_ok` (2026-11-12; the 22:05 ruling, item 3): BORROW-002, as their cast twin's control `cw_F_tc_ok` (job B's).
// `m_P_mcc_ok`, `mv_P_dc_ok`, `ms_P_dc_ok` (2026-11-12; the 22:05 ruling, item 3): BORROW-009, as their cast twin's control `m_P_cwc_ok` (Ailp-team_103's).
// `m_P_mtc_ok`, `mv_P_tc_ok`, `ms_P_tc_ok` (2026-11-12; the 22:05 ruling, item 3): BORROW-002 and BORROW-009, as their cast twin's control `cw_P_tc_ok` (job B's).
// `argt_par_ok` (2026-11-12; the 03:58 ruling, item 4): the result over a parameter's pointer is kept in a local, and the local is handed to `keep(pb, t)`, which
// stores it. BORROW-015, as its twin `argt_par_cw8_ok` (nitpick-compiler_108's, as the cell is: the family's call discarded and `t` made by the casts through
// `wild int8->` alone) on c9e3095 and on main.
// `est_lprp2_par_ok` (2026-11-12; the 04:24 addendum, item 3): the destination is a local pointer made from a local's address and re-pointed at the parameter's
// pointer before the call. BORROW-002, as its cast twin `est_lprp2_par_cw_ok` on every build (both management_39's).
// DEF-639's, TWO (LOW, fails closed). The result over a parameter's pointer is cast back from `wild` in the argument itself and handed to `keep(pb, v)`, which stores
// it. With this row the family's result carries its destination's view, as a user call's result carries its argument's, so the pointer the call makes meets
// DEF-585's rule: BORROW-015. A cast in the argument makes no call, so each cast twin runs 67 on every build. A user function that makes the same pointer is
// refused by c9e3095 and by main with BORROW-015 too (`arg_pp_mk0_ok`, `arg_pp_mkw_ok`, management_39's). Both cells go back to an `_ok` file when DEF-639 lifts.
// `arg_pp_ok` (2026-11-12; DEF-639; the 03:58 ruling, item 4): the family copies nothing, the parameter's pointer onto itself. Cast twins `arg_pp_cw_ok` and `arg_pp_cw8_ok`; user-call twins `arg_pp_mk0_ok` and `arg_pp_mkw_ok`.
// `arg_par_ok` (2026-11-12; DEF-639; the 03:58 ruling, item 4): the family copies a local into the parameter's pointee. Cast twins `arg_par_cw_ok` and `arg_par_cw8_ok`; the user-call twins are `arg_pp_ok`'s.
// DEF-652's, NINE (LOW, fails closed, made by this row). The family's verdict asks rule A over every argument, and a second look over the destination alone. Here
// the source is a local's address, so an argument borrows from the frame, and the destination is the caller's storage or a heap block, in a form the second look
// doesn't read as a parameter's alone. Nothing escapes. The nine go back to an `_ok` file when DEF-652 lifts.
// THE FIRST SIX (nitpick-compiler_109's, as their cast twins are): `est_` stores the result into a slice parameter's element (BORROW-002) and `ret_` hands it
// back (BORROW-001). Each cast twin runs 67 on every build.
// `est_wp_par_ok` (2026-11-12; DEF-652; the 04:24 addendum, item 1): the destination is a member of a local struct that holds the parameter's pointer, `w.p`. Cast twin `est_wp_par_cw_ok`.
// `ret_wp_par_ok` (2026-11-12; DEF-652; the 04:24 addendum, item 1): the same member, the result handed back. Cast twin `ret_wp_par_cw_ok`.
// `est_la_par_ok` (2026-11-12; DEF-652; the 04:24 addendum, item 1): the destination is an element of a local array that holds the parameter's pointer, `la[0i64]`. Cast twin `est_la_par_cw_ok`.
// `ret_la_par_ok` (2026-11-12; DEF-652; the 04:24 addendum, item 1): the same element, the result handed back. Cast twin `ret_la_par_cw_ok`.
// `est_dq_par_ok` (2026-11-12; DEF-652; the 04:24 addendum, item 1): the destination is the pointee of a pointer-to-pointer parameter, `<-pq`. Cast twin `est_dq_par_cw_ok`.
// `ret_dq_par_ok` (2026-11-12; DEF-652; the 04:24 addendum, item 1): the same pointee, the result handed back. Cast twin `ret_dq_par_cw_ok`.
// THREE MORE, BY TWO MORE FORMS (Ailp-team_111's, as their twins are; the 04:58 ruling, item 3, named on each line by its title). Each `_cw_ok` twin makes the
// same copy as a statement and then uses the destination; each `_cw0_ok` twin makes no call, writes the pointee and casts the destination through `wild int8->`.
// Each twin runs 67 on every build.
// `pr_hf_ret_ok` (2026-11-12; DEF-652; "Row 3's fresh attack is ruled", Thu 11-12 04:58 EST, item 3): the destination is `hs.p`, a heap block held in a local struct's field; the result handed back. BORROW-001. Twins `pr_hf_ret_cw_ok`, `pr_hf_ret_cw0_ok`.
// `pr_hf_pst_ok` (2026-11-12; DEF-652; "Row 3's fresh attack is ruled", Thu 11-12 04:58 EST, item 3): the same field; the result stored through a pointer parameter. BORROW-002. Twins `pr_hf_pst_cw_ok`, `pr_hf_pst_cw0_ok`.
// `pr_se_ret_ok` (2026-11-12; DEF-652; "Row 3's fresh attack is ruled", Thu 11-12 04:58 EST, item 3): the destination is `ps[0i64]`, a slice-of-pointers parameter's element; the result handed back. BORROW-001. Twins `pr_se_ret_cw_ok`, `pr_se_ret_cw0_ok`.
error:E5;
struct:K_m_L_mc = { int64:k; };
struct:H_m_L_mc = { List<int64>:l; int64:k; K_m_L_mc:kk; };
func:mk_m_L_mc = List<int64>() never fails { List<int64>:l = raw list_init::<int64>(1i64); drop list_push(@l, 1i64); pass l; };
func:mkh_m_L_mc = H_m_L_mc() never fails { pass H_m_L_mc{ l: raw mk_m_L_mc(), k: 2i64, kk: K_m_L_mc{ k: 3i64 } }; };
func:growh_m_L_mc = int64(H_m_L_mc->:w) never fails { loop(0i64, 64i64, 1i64) { drop list_push(@w.l, 1i64); } pass 7i64; };
func:fp_m_L_mc = int64->(H_m_L_mc->:q) never fails { pass @((<-q).l[0i64]); };
struct:HPZ_m_L_mc = { H_m_L_mc->:lp; int64:z; };
struct:SQN_m_L_mc = { HPZ_m_L_mc->:q; int64:z; };
struct:SQZ_m_L_mc = { HPZ_m_L_mc->:q; HPZ_m_L_mc:h; };
struct:SQZO_m_L_mc = { HPZ_m_L_mc->:q; HPZ_m_L_mc:h; List<int64>:own; };
struct:SQO_m_L_mc = { HPZ_m_L_mc->:q; List<int64>:own; };
struct:SQ2_m_L_mc = { SQZ_m_L_mc:iv; int64:z; };
enum:ESQ_m_L_mc = { A(SQO_m_L_mc); Z; };
enum:ESZ_m_L_mc = { A(SQZO_m_L_mc); Z; };
func:idp_m_L_mc = HPZ_m_L_mc->(HPZ_m_L_mc->:q) never fails { pass q; };
func:setq_m_L_mc = int64(HPZ_m_L_mc->->:pq, HPZ_m_L_mc->:v) never fails { <-pq = v; pass 0i64; };
func:swapq_m_L_mc = int64(HPZ_m_L_mc->->:a, HPZ_m_L_mc->->:c) never fails { HPZ_m_L_mc->:t = <-a; <-a = <-c; <-c = t; pass 0i64; };
func:mksq_m_L_mc = SQN_m_L_mc(HPZ_m_L_mc->:p) never fails { pass SQN_m_L_mc{ q: p, z: 1i64 }; };
func:setlp_m_L_mc = int64(HPZ_m_L_mc->:p, H_m_L_mc->:v) never fails { (<-p).lp = v; pass 0i64; };
func:cpq_m_L_mc = int64(HPZ_m_L_mc->->:a, HPZ_m_L_mc->->:c) never fails { <-a = <-c; pass 0i64; };
func:cpq2_m_L_mc = int64(HPZ_m_L_mc->->:a, HPZ_m_L_mc->->:c) never fails { HPZ_m_L_mc->:v = <-c; <-a = v; pass 0i64; };
func:g2_m_L_mc = int64(HPZ_m_L_mc->:mp0, H_m_L_mc->:hq, H_m_L_mc->:hz, H_m_L_mc->:h0p, int64:n) never fails {
    int64:acc = 0i64;
